Here is the JavaScript code to sort an array of objects by a numeric field called `count`.

The `sort()` method accepts a comparator function. When sorting numbers, you must return the result of the subtraction (`a - b`) to ensure ascending order.

```javascript
// 1. Define the array of objects with the provided counts
const items = [
  { name: "Item A", count: 25 },
  { name: "Item B", count: 32 },
  { name: "Item C", count: 38 }
];

// 2. Sort the array in ascending order based on the 'count' field
items.sort((a, b) => a.count - b.count);

// 3. Output the result to the console
console.log("Sorted Array:", items);

// Optional: Verify the order
console.log("First item count:", items[0].count); // Should be 25
console.log("Last item count:", items[items.length - 1].count); // Should be 38
```

### Output
When you run this code, the array will be arranged from the lowest count to the highest:

```javascript
[
  { name: 'Item A', count: 25 },
  { name: 'Item B', count: 32 },
  { name: 'Item C', count: 38 }
]
```

### Note on Descending Order
If you need to sort from **highest to lowest**, simply swap the operands in the subtraction:

```javascript
items.sort((a, b) => b.count - a.count);
```
